CRISPR editing can produce off-target mutations — cuts at unintended genomic sites — with consequences that may not be apparent for years or generations.
Gene drives (self-propagating genetic modifications) can spread through wild populations exponentially; a mistake cannot be recalled — extinction or ecosystem disruption could be permanent.
The 2018 He Jiankui scandal produced two children with germline CRISPR edits of unknown long-term consequence, demonstrating that without regulation, irreversible experiments on humans will occur.
